// EXPECT: error
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: cannot `move` `old`: `Anchor` is `NoMove`
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: in the instantiation `Vector<Anchor, GlobalAllocator>.swap_out`
//
// A DIAGNOSTIC LIVING ONLY IN A DEMANDED-BUT-NEVER-EMITTED INSTANCE'S BODY
// FIRES IN AN EXECUTABLE BUILD — design 218c Amendment A5(a)'s pin, and the
// only thing that survives of A5 after the Sep-1 SPLICE-ALL ruling.
//
// A2(b) would have aligned CHECKING with EMISSION: an instance demanded but
// never emitted in an executable would have been registered, depth-checked and
// effect-validated and never instance-checked, so a diagnostic living only
// there would surface in a `-c` object and not in the program. The user
// overrode the measurement that selected it: checking aligns with EXISTENCE,
// `-c` and `-o` report identically, and this is the test that says so.
//
// `wire` is never called, so design 168's reachability strip emits no body for
// it and none for the `Vector<Anchor, GlobalAllocator>` methods it demands. The
// instance still EXISTS — the fixpoint's roots are every concrete declaration —
// so it is materialized and checked, and the refusal below is what a
// `Vector` at a `NoMove` element type owes: `Vector` RELOCATES its elements on
// a growth and on a `swap_out`, which is the containment design 188 states and
// which nothing had ever enforced through a generic.

struct Anchor { n: Int }
extension Anchor: NoCopy {}
extension Anchor: NoMove {}

func wire() -> Int {
    var v = Vector<Anchor>()
    let _ = v.swap_out(0, Anchor(n: 1))
    v.len()
}

func main() {
    print("nothing calls `wire`")
}
